 "text": "The péist is a monstrous serpent or reptile in Irish folklore. It is also called the bruckee. It appears in legends where Saint Patrick overcomes it. The péist lives in fresh water, often a lake, and dies only when submerged. It is also called the Ollipheist. These beasts are common in the Burren of County Clare, where place names like Poul na Peasta mean water dragon's lair.",
